    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>Système de cote ÉnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="5" build="8">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.5b8</English>
                <French>v15.5b8</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16</English>
                <French>v1.16</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage is occurring around your window frames, around your doors, and through the portholes that have been installed in your walls. Eliminating some of the portholes may also result in additional savings that I am not able to model. 



I recommend you replace your bathroom ventilation window with an ENERGY STAR® rated exhaust fan. I also checked your range hood fan, and while it appears to be working well, it does need to be cleaned. I also recommend that when it is time to replace this fan, you select an ENERGY STAR® rated exhaust fan.



Some of the other upgrades suggested in this report will also reduce your air leakage, For example, upgrading your furnace may allow you to eliminate the combustion air duct in your utility room. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 12% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $155&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <foundation>

Your crawlspace walls appear to be 2x4 framing with minimal insulation. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R12) fibreglass batt insulation between the studs of your crawlspace walls.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 4 inches (R212) fibreglass batt insulation to the crawlspace walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $35&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the crawl space wall area, including the header area. The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</foundation>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, and you indicated an interest in replacing them, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Although I have not modelled the savings, I also recommend replacing both your doors with ENERGY STAR® qualified doors rated for climate zone 3.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all your windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $305&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;10.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have an instantaneous propane fired hot water heater with a default energy factor of 0.83. This hot water heater is currently in good shape and I would not recommend replacing it at this time. 



However, I have modelled replacing the hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing propane-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your current instantaneous hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $11&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current propane fired furnace is a condensing propane fired furnace that was originally rated at 92.1% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. A direct vent unit may also allow you to eliminate the combustion air duct coming up into your utility room. 



I would not recommend replacing your furnace at this time, unless there are other reasons to do so. If and when you do replace the furnace, consult with an HVAC expert to see if the combustion air can be eliminated. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $125&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



I have also modelled the savings you may be achieving by using your woodstove to meet 50% of the heating load on the main floor of your home. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your woodstove to meet 50% of the heating load of the main floor of your home, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $335&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and increase your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;29.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. Should you choose to replace your woodstove with a more efficient unit, you could qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program and achieve additional savings.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely. The short and dark days of winter provide little or no usable energy, depending on the latitude in the NWT. However, the long hours of sunlight in spring and summer can provide usable heat and electricity for up to eight months of the year.  As prices continue to drop, solar PV panels are becoming competitive with the cost of diesel generation. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $950&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;11.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt; GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system. You would be eligible for 1/3 of the purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year. Applicants are strongly encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;

</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>

I recommend you replace your toilet. When replacing your toilet, purchase 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilets. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing your toilet with a new 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilet, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;ECM Pumps&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to learn the systems usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$ 175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;





&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $6.58 per bag



